<BaseLayout
  title="Kuwait Ports — Shuwaikh & Shuaiba Intelligence: China Routing, Transit & Clearance (September 2026)"
  description="Kuwait ports intelligence for China freight: Shuwaikh (KWSWK) vs Shuaiba (KWSHB), port roles, China transit windows, 5% duty clearance and the two-port routing decision."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Kuwait Ports' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">September 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Kuwait Ports: Shuwaikh &amp; Shuaiba intelligence for China freight</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A port-intelligence page for shippers routing China freight into Kuwait. This page explains
        the Shuwaikh-vs-Shuaiba decision, the two UN/LOCODEs, the port roles and hinterland logic,
        how long China ocean moves take, what the destination cost stack looks like, and the 5% duty
        clearance rules that apply at the Kuwait gate.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified port codes and the GCC duty/VAT baseline from indicative
        routing and fee notes. Treat every LOW-confidence port, transit and fee note as a planning
        guide, and verify with the Kuwait port authority or your carrier before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Ports</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Shuwaikh / Shuaiba <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">UN/LOCODE</span>
          <span class="fact-value">{shuwaikhUnlocode} / {shuaibaUnlocode} <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Port type</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Seaports <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">China sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Typical 24 days <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">General VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">None <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Kuwait’s two-port map matters for China freight</h2>
    <p>
      Kuwait is a compact, oil-led GCC economy, but it lacks the single-gateway simplicity of Qatar’s
      Hamad Port or the volume of Dubai’s Jebel Ali. Before any China-to-Kuwait consignment is booked,
      the importer faces a genuine routing question: <strong>Shuwaikh or Shuaiba?</strong> The choice
      changes inland distance, cargo fit and destination-fee exposure.
    </p>
    <p>
      The commonly quoted commercial gateway is <strong>Shuwaikh</strong>, close to Kuwait City, while
      <strong>Shuaiba</strong> is the port to consider for industrial, petrochemical, bulk and
      project-linked cargo in the south. Both have their own UN/LOCODEs, but the verified snapshot
      does not publish their berths, draft, cranes, throughput, operators or fees. Route logically,
      then request the operational facts from the port authority and carrier.
    </p>
    <p>
      The statutory side is simpler: Kuwait uses the GCC <strong>5% customs duty on CIF value</strong>
      baseline, with no general VAT. The real landed-cost variance therefore comes from port choice,
      terminal charges, clearance discipline and demurrage/detention — the areas this page keeps visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Kuwait port parameters</h2>
      <p>
        Use this table as an orientation, not a terminal directory. The country, port codes and GCC
        duty/VAT baseline are the verified anchors; the role split, operator, berths and throughput
        are marked LOW because the verified snapshot does not publish Kuwait port-authority details.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'parameter', label: 'Parameter' },
          { key: 'value', label: 'Value' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={parameterRows}
        caption="Kuwait port parameters — UN/LOCODEs and the GCC duty/VAT baseline are the HIGH/MEDIUM anchors; operational and role details are LOW confidence and require port-authority confirmation."
      />
      <SourceList sources={portFactSources} title="Sources — Kuwait port parameters" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Capacity-sensitive cargo:</strong> if your consignment depends on a specific berth,
        draft, crane capability, heavy-lift availability, terminal window or product-handling class,
        request the current schedule and restrictions from the Kuwait port authority or your
        forwarder before booking. The verified snapshot does not supply berth-by-berth constraints.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. China to Kuwait port transit windows</h2>
    <p>
      Transit into Kuwait is a planning range, not a promise. The route data gives Shuwaikh a typical
      24 days with a 15–30 day range, LOW confidence. Shuaiba sea transit and Kuwait air transit are
      not published, so the table keeps those lines explicit instead of borrowing another corridor’s
      figure.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'origin', label: 'Origin' },
        { key: 'service', label: 'Service' },
        { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={transitRows}
      caption="Indicative transit windows only — verify with the relevant carrier before relying on these figures."
    />
